  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 AM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis shows low sliding through northwest\nManitoba, with frontal boundary dropping south through\nManitoba/Eastern Saskatchewan into Northwest North Dakota. \nUpper level analysis places flattened ridge over the central \nCONUS, with potent short wave sliding from Saskatchewan into \nManitoba. Over our area, winds have been picking up a bit behind\nthe pre-frontal trough, otherwise quiet weather continues. \n\nFor today, flattened ridge will remain over the area, as front\ncontinues to make its way to the southeast early in the day.\nTemperatures will be cooler than yesterday, though overall\nremaining above average. Surface high will settle over the area\ntonight in the wake of the cold front with winds going light. \nThis will allow temperatures to fall into the 30s, with the \ncoldest temperatures expected to be over areas that have \nalready experienced a freeze. \n\nFor the first part of the week, a Pacific low will drop \nsoutherly along the west coast, loitering off the California\nshore mid-week. This will keep a general ridging pattern over\nour area, which amplifies some mid-week. Therefore, conditions\nwill remain dry with seasonably mild temperatures, which\ngradually warm some mid-week with the amplifying ridge. \n\nFor the later part of the week, latest ensemble guidance are in\nagreement with bringing the low to the east, and opening it as\nit pushes over the Rockies, resulting in a surface low that will\nlift through the plains. Although timing varies some with these\nfeatures, the overall pattern will be active resulting in low to\nmedium chances for rain and possibly some thunderstorms for \nFriday and Saturday.",
